# The battlegrounds' lore in chat (Living Server 1.2, owner 2026-11-15: "We need full lore for the battlegrounds written,
# and it needs to be REALLY good"). The lore itself is in data/lore/locations/proving-fields.json and the four
# battleground files; this file holds what the bots say of it.
#
# - Four storyteller legends (AutonomousTales.Legends: no boss, no raid, no fireside), one a battleground. Abermenai is
#   told in every realm; Thidranki is Midgard's, Murdaigean Hibernia's and Caledonia Albion's, each in the realm whose
#   tradition names the keep. The teller writes whole sentences with capitals and stops, as in tale_atlantis; the chat
#   pass keeps them and sends a line of more than 14 words as two, at a sentence or the comma nearest its middle.
# - The rumours that follow each legend (topic rumour_<lore id>, LoreLegends.RumoursAfter: no @needs), which are also
#   ordinary musings in /faction.
# - A few lines said in the field itself (topic battlegrounds, @needs in_battleground and the field).
# New writing. Myth adapted from public-domain sources (the Mabinogi, Cad Goddeu, the Thidrandi tale, the Fianna's
# tests in Keating); nothing is quoted.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Abermenai: the one who waited (every realm)
@kind debate
@realm *
@topic tale_abermenai
@levels 1-50
@scene tale_abermenai
@role A type=*
@role B type=*
@role C type=Socialite,Explorer
@role D type=*
@role E type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role F type=Leveler,Crafter,Explorer
A: {name_b} you on? tell the abermenai one, the mother at the teleporter | {name_b}, do the one about the woman who waited
C: oh, the abermenai one. hush everyone | quiet in faction for a bit, {name_b} is telling abermenai
?E: is this the sad one | here we go, the mum story
D: go on {name_b}, i never heard how it ends | please {name_b}, the new ones need to hear this one
B: In the summer they opened Abermenai there was a woman on the frontier with one son, and he had just turned fifteen.
B: She had held a wall for twenty years, and she knew better than anyone what fifteen is worth when the other side comes over it.
B: On the night of his first muster she walked him to the teleporter, and when he was sent she asked to be sent after him.
B: The teleporter would not. She had too much war in her for that field, and he told her so as politely as he tells everyone.
B: She made him try three times. Then she stood by the teleporter all night in her mail, facing the way her son had gone.
~ 8
D: so what happened to the kid? | did he make it?
B: He went down onto the sand at dusk with eleven others his own age, and the renegades came out of the old fort to meet them.
B: Somewhere on the flats below the walls he fell. He told her afterward that it was very cold, and then it was morning.
B: He woke at our own portal keep, whole, with sand in his hair, and at dawn he came back out of the teleporter to her.
?F: what did she say? | did she yell at him?
B: Nothing. He had a renegade's belt he was very proud of, and she took it off him to carry, and she carried it all the way home.
B: She never once asked him how it went. He went back the next night, and every night that summer, and she stood there every night.
B: The year after there were four of them standing there. By the third summer the teleporters had stopped asking them to go home.
B: Go and look on the first night of a muster. There are always a few grown soldiers by the teleporter with nowhere to be.
~ 25
F: ...i always thought they were just afk | ...huh. i thought those were people afk
A: ty {name_b}. that one gets me every time | thanks {name_b}, i needed that one tonight
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Thidranki: Thidrand and the winter nights (Midgard)
@kind debate
@realm mid
@topic tale_thidranki
@levels 1-50
@scene tale_thidranki
@role A type=*
@role B type=*
@role C type=Socialite,Explorer
@role D type=*
@role E type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role F type=Leveler,Crafter,Explorer
A: {name_b} you on? tell thidrand, the farm under the faste | {name_b}, do the winter nights one for the new ones
C: hush in faction, {name_b} is telling thidrand | everyone quiet, this one is old
?E: the farm story again lol | here we go, nine ladies on horses
D: go on {name_b}, i only know theres a farm under the keep | please {name_b}, i never heard all of it
B: The hill where the Faste stands was a farm once, and the farmer's son was Thidrand. He was eighteen, and everyone liked him.
B: At the winter nights feast there was a seer among the guests, and he said that nobody should go out that night, whoever knocked.
B: Late, when the household was asleep on the benches, someone knocked at the door three times.
B: Thidrand thought it shameful to leave a guest out in the cold, so he took his sword and went to the door.
~ 8
D: who was it? | so who was knocking?
B: There was nobody at the door. But nine women in black were riding into the homefield from the north with their swords drawn.
B: And from the south came nine more, in white, on white horses, riding as hard as they could.
B: The ones in black reached him first. They found him at first light by the woodpile, and he lived long enough to say what he saw.
?F: who were they? | so what were the ones in white?
B: The seer said both were spirits of the house. The black knew a change was coming, and would have their due before it came.
B: The white belonged to what was coming. They had meant to save him, and they had been too late.
B: The family left the hill in the spring. The tomte and the bwca stayed, and they have quarrelled over that farm ever since.
B: Nobody who has fallen on that hill since has stayed dead. The ones in white are never late there now.
~ 25
F: ...going to leave porridge out for the tomte tonight | ...never late there now. huh
E: ok, that one is good | fine. that one i like
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Murdaigean: the run and the knots (Hibernia)
@kind debate
@realm hib
@topic tale_murdaigean
@levels 1-50
@scene tale_murdaigean
@role A type=*
@role B type=*
@role C type=Socialite,Explorer
@role D type=*
@role E type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role F type=Leveler,Crafter,Explorer
A: {name_b} are you on? tell the murdaigean one, the knots | {name_b}, do the run through murd for the new ones
C: whisht everyone, {name_b} is telling the wood | quiet in faction please, the murdaigean one
?E: the knots story? go on then | here we go, braid your hair everyone
D: go on {name_b}, i keep seeing those cords on the trees | please {name_b}, i never knew what the knots were
B: Long before the realm war the Fianna tried their young in that wood, and the dun at the heart of it was where the trial ended.
B: They braided your hair and gave you the start of one tree, and then the whole company came after you through the wood.
B: If they caught you, you had failed. If a braid came loose on a branch, or a dry stick cracked under your foot, you had failed.
B: You had to leap a branch as high as your brow, go under one as low as your knee, and pull a thorn from your foot at a run.
~ 8
D: did anyone ever reach the dun? | so who made it to the fort?
B: Hardly anyone. So wherever they caught you they tied a knot in a cord on the nearest tree, to show how far you had come.
B: A boy who was caught halfway would come back as a grown man, and find his knot, and stand under it a while without a word.
B: The Fianna went away a long time ago. The cords stayed on the trees, and the spiders took them over, one knot at a time.
?F: wait, the webs are the cords? | so the webs in murd are the old knots?
B: Some of them. On the oldest trees, now and then, there is a knot in a web where no spider would ever tie one.
B: The name of the dun says fortress twice in the old tongue. The filidh say that was so a runner who reached it would believe it.
B: It was never built to keep anyone out. It was the one place in that wood where nobody was chasing you.
~ 25
F: ...going to look for knots next time | ...i always thought those were just webs
A: ty {name_b}, lovely telling | thanks {name_b}, i never knew that one
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Caledonia: the battle of the trees (Albion)
@kind debate
@realm alb
@topic tale_caledon
@levels 1-50
@scene tale_caledon
@role A type=*
@role B type=*
@role C type=Socialite,Explorer
@role D type=*
@role E type=NoLifer,Daredevil
@role F type=Leveler,Crafter,Explorer
A: {name_b} you on? tell the battle of the trees for the new ones | {name_b}, tell them why the trees in cale stand in ranks
C: hush in faction, {name_b} is telling the trees | quiet everyone, this one is very old
?E: the walking trees again lol | here we go, the grovewood
D: go on {name_b}, those trees keep killing me | please {name_b}, i want to know why they hate us
B: Long before Arthur, a man of Gwynedd stole a white roebuck and a hound pup out of Annwn.
B: Arawn came up out of the earth after them with a host, and the thief's brother was Gwydion, the best enchanter in the west.
B: Gwydion had no host to meet him with, so he went into the wood of Celidon and sang, and the trees pulled up their roots.
B: They came down the hill in ranks. The alders went in the front line, and the willow and the rowan were slow to form up.
B: The birch was too proud to hurry. The oak came last of all, and the ground shook under it the whole way down.
~ 8
D: so did the trees win? | who won?
B: Arawn had a champion who could not be beaten while his name was hidden, and the trees broke on him all day.
B: Then Gwydion saw sprigs of alder on the man's shield, and guessed that his name was Bran, and called it out, and he fell.
B: Arawn went back under the earth. His people stayed in the hills above the wood, and they keep those hills for him still.
?F: and the trees? | so why are the trees still standing there?
B: Gwydion meant to sing them back to sleep once the day was done. Before he could, word came that he was wanted at home.
B: A boy of his house had no name and could not get one, and Gwydion walked him down the shore to Aber Menai to see to it.
B: He never went back to Celidon. The grovewood are standing in their ranks yet, facing the hills, waiting on the second song.
B: The boy got his name in the end.
~ 25
F: ...aber menai. as in abermenai? | wait. aber menai, like the beach?
A: ty {name_b}. i will be nicer to the trees | thanks {name_b}, nobody does the old ones like you
@end

